<h2>File by file</h2>
<h3><code>env.ts</code>: configuration</h3>
<p>Declares every binding (<code>DB</code>, <code>ASSETS</code>), variable (<code>APP_ORIGIN</code>, <code>ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>PUSH_ENDPOINT_HOSTS</code>, limits) and secret (<code>ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, <code>TOKEN_ENC_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY</code>, <code>VAPID_SUBJECT</code>). <code>assertConfigured()</code> answers <em>503 "Cloud edition is not configured: missing …"</em> (names only, never values) until everything is set, so a half-configured deployment cannot accept sign-ins.</p>

<h3><code>http.ts</code>: the web plumbing</h3>
<ul>
  <li><code>json()</code>, <code>redirect()</code> add security headers (<code>nosniff</code>, <code>DENY</code> framing, no referrer, <code>no-store</code>, HSTS).</li>
  <li><code>errorResponse()</code> turns known errors into clean JSON, and unknown ones into a generic 500 (the details go to the log, not to the user).</li>
  <li><code>cookie()</code> always sets <code>Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax</code> for <code>__Host-</code> cookies.</li>
  <li><code>assertSameOrigin()</code>: the CSRF guard (Origin, Sec-Fetch-Site, <code>X-JBD: 1</code>).</li>
  <li><code>readJson()</code>: refuses bodies over 16 KB and invalid JSON.</li>
  <li><code>rateLimit()</code>: a per-isolate token counter (a speed bump, not a global quota).</li>
</ul>

<h3><code>crypto.ts</code>: small, careful helpers</h3>
<p>base64url encoding, random tokens (<code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>), SHA-256, constant-time comparison, and <code>encryptJson</code>/<code>decryptJson</code> with AES-256-GCM, a random 12-byte IV per encryption and additional authenticated data <code>grant:&lt;userId&gt;</code>. See <a href="security-crypto.html">The cryptography, simply</a>.</p>

<h3><code>auth.ts</code>: identity and tokens</h3>
<ul>
  <li><code>startLogin()</code>: per-IP sign-in limit (D1), random <code>state</code> in an HttpOnly cookie, redirect to <code>auth.atlassian.com/authorize</code> with four scopes.</li>
  <li><code>finishLogin()</code>: checks state (constant time), exchanges the code, checks the granted scopes, reads <code>/me</code> and <code>accessible-resources</code>, then in <em>one batch</em> upserts the user, stores the encrypted grant, replaces the sites, creates settings and a session.</li>
  <li><code>getSession()</code>: hashes the cookie, finds the session; enforces 30-day absolute and 7-day idle expiry; slides the idle timer at most once an hour.</li>
  <li><code>accessToken()</code>: returns a valid access token, refreshing with a lease and a version check (<a href="flow-token-refresh.html">details</a>).</li>
  <li><code>cloudApi()</code>: the cloud implementation of the core's <code>JiraApi</code>, retrying once after forcing a refresh on 401.</li>
</ul>

<h3><code>api.ts</code>: the endpoints</h3>
<p>One router (<code>handleApi</code>) with a <code>switch</code> on <code>"METHOD /path"</code>. Sign-in routes come first (they are top-level navigations), then the CSRF check, then <code>/api/config</code> (works signed-out), then <code>requireSession()</code> and a per-user rate limit for everything else. All endpoints are listed in the <a href="api-reference.html">API reference</a>.</p>

<h3><code>monitor.ts</code>: the cron engine</h3>
<p><code>runTick()</code> loads due users in one query, checks each (<code>checkUser</code>), defers whoever does not fit, retries pending pushes, runs the privacy report at minute :37 and housekeeping at minute :13. Each user check is incremental and writes nothing when idle. Complete walkthrough: <a href="flow-cron.html">The background check</a> and <a href="jarvis-engine.html">JARVIS engine</a>.</p>

<h3><code>budget.ts</code>: staying under 50 subrequests</h3>
<pre><code>export class Budget {
  used = 0;
  constructor(public readonly size: number) {}
  get left() { return this.size - this.used; }
  has(n: number) { return this.left &gt;= n; }
  take(n = 1) { if (this.used + n &gt; this.size) throw new BudgetExhausted(); this.used += n; }
}</code></pre>
<p>Every fetch to Atlassian or a push service and every D1 call calls <code>budget.take()</code> first. If a user's work does not fit, <code>BudgetExhausted</code> is caught, the user is marked deferred, and nothing half-done is committed.</p>

<h3><code>push.ts</code>: Web Push</h3>
<p><code>endpointAllowed()</code> (HTTPS + allow-listed push hosts), <code>vapidJwt()</code> (ES256-signed, cached 12 hours per push origin), <code>encryptPayload()</code> (RFC 8291) and <code>sendPush()</code> (maps 404/410 to "gone" so dead subscriptions are deleted). See <a href="web-push.html">Service workers and Web Push</a>.</p>

<h3><code>privacy.ts</code>: Personal Data Reporting</h3>
<p>Lists every stored account id, reports up to 90 per request to Atlassian, erases on <em>closed</em>, clears on <em>updated</em>. See <a href="privacy-lifecycle.html">Privacy and the data life cycle</a>.</p>

<h3><code>cloud/public/sw.js</code>: the service worker</h3>
<p>Not part of the Worker (it runs in the browser), but deployed with it. Handles <code>push</code> (show notifications, forward events to open tabs), <code>notificationclick</code> (focus or open the dashboard at the bug), and <code>pushsubscriptionchange</code> (re-register).</p>

<h2>Configuration file: <code>wrangler.jsonc</code></h2>
<pre><code>{
  "name": "jira-bug-dashboard-cloud",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-11-01",
  "assets": { "directory": "./dist-assets", "binding": "ASSETS", "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/auth/*"] },
  "env": {
    "dev":        { "vars": { … mock URLs … }, "d1_databases": [ … local … ], "triggers": { "crons": ["* * * * *"] } },
    "production": { "vars": { "APP_ORIGIN": "https://…workers.dev", "ATLASSIAN_CLIENT_ID": "…", … },
                    "d1_databases": [{ "binding": "DB", "database_name": "jbd-cloud", "database_id": "…" }],
                    "triggers": { "crons": ["* * * * *"] } }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Each environment has its own bindings, variables and triggers (they are not inherited). Secrets are not in this file at all. More in <a href="cf-storage.html">D1, assets, cron, secrets and environments</a>.</p>

<h2>What the Worker never does</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Return or log tokens (the logs show request URLs with the OAuth code and state redacted by Cloudflare).</li>
  <li>Trust a user id or site id from the client: the site must belong to the session's user.</li>
  <li>Store Jira issue contents beyond what monitoring and history need.</li>
  <li>Keep state between runs in memory (except harmless caches such as the VAPID JWT).</li>
</ul>
